UNIT canonical_text
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs canonicalText over sirLines — the binding normal form every PRIMARY-SHA256 hashes
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG canonical_text(text: text) -> text
ENVELOPE well-formed text (Unicode scalar values). Inside it the function is total. Text holding a lone surrogate is OUT in
  every language: the package throws on it, and no language is graded there. Non-text input is OUT.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Normalize the formatting of a document, line by line, and nothing else: unify line breaks, drop the byte order
  marks among a line's leading spaces and tabs, drop trailing spaces and tabs, drop blank lines, collapse any indentation of spaces and tabs to
  exactly two spaces. Every other character is kept as it is. The line cutting of R1–R2 is the same one the SIR parser
  uses, so two texts with equal results parse alike.
BEHAVIOR
R1 The text is cut into lines at every BREAK, scanning left to right (grammar BREAK): a CR immediately followed by LF is
  one break, a CR alone is a break, an LF alone is a break. So LF then CR is two breaks. The line separator (2028), the
  paragraph separator (2029), next line (0085), vertical tab and form feed are NOT breaks: they are content. CITED FIDELITY package
R2 Each line first loses every byte order mark (hex FEFF) inside its LEADING RUN: the longest prefix made only of PAD
  characters and marks, in any mix and order (it may be empty). The PAD characters of that run stay, in their order;
  only its marks go. A mark after the first character that is neither PAD nor a mark is content. Then the line loses
  its trailing run of PAD characters. Every whitespace other than PAD at the end of a line (no-break space,
  ideographic space, the separators of R1) is content, and so is any pad character before it. CITED FIDELITY package
R3 A line that is empty after R2 is dropped. A line made only of other whitespace (a no-break space alone) is not empty
  and is kept.
R4 A kept line that starts with a PAD character has its whole leading run of PAD characters replaced by exactly two
  spaces. A line starting with any other character is unchanged; a no-break space at column 0 is content, not
  indentation.
R5 The kept lines, in order, are joined with LF and one final LF is added. When no line is kept the result is the EMPTY
  text (not a lone LF). CITED FIDELITY package
PINNED charset PAD = hex code points 0020 0009 CITED R2 R4 — space and tab, nothing else
PINNED grammar BREAK = CR LF | CR | LF, the longest alternative first at each position CITED R1
INVARIANT the result is empty or ends with LF; it holds no CR; no line of it is empty or ends with space or tab; a line
  is either two spaces followed by a character that is neither PAD nor a byte order mark, or starts with such a
  character. Hence the function is idempotent: applied to its own result it returns that result unchanged.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN no text throws. Malformed SIR is not a concept here: orphan lines, comments, unknown directives and
  non-directive lines all pass through under R1–R5 unchanged in content.
ORACLE value -> oracles/canonical_text.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 07d5e3c1abe2557680668091b3d5998ffab3d87651bbb845fa5ff7da178014d3
SCOPE IN: R1–R5 over well-formed text. OUT: the parse and the canonical print (separate units), the sha256 of the result, lone
  surrogates.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (MIT), cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs: sirLines, canonicalText; seam selfhost/seams/canonical_text.mjs. Cold
  cut 2026-10-26 into the v0.5 contract register from the pinned authority snapshot (round 1 of the self-treatment
  pilot, replacing canonical_print); re-cut 2026-10-27 for round 5 (FINDINGS #76: sirLines drops the marks after a
  line's indentation too); re-cut 2026-10-27 for round 5b on 6199e2cc8b75 (FINDINGS #99: every
  mark in the leading run goes, so one pass reaches the fixed point).
